{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/hovkph","slideIndex":3,"text":"My first prediction experiment used current and lagged weather information to predict tomorrow’s temperature. I trained on the earlier seventy-five percent of dates and tested on the final quarter. Chicago’s linear regression and neural network both had average errors of about 1.46 degrees. In San Diego, linear regression scored 0.63, compared with 0.68 for the network. Greater model complexity did not automatically improve this forecast.\n\nPlanned narration: 30 seconds.\nThis slide belongs to the five-minute main presentation.\n\nSource: Saved notebook cell 62 results. 542 training examples and 181 later test examples."}
