{"kind":"image","id":"im/qdorql07","slideIndex":7,"position":{"left":60,"top":167.67,"width":1160,"height":386.67},"fit":"contain","name":"","alt":"week6_regularization_results/alpha_validation_mae.png"}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/cmq5nd","slideIndex":7,"text":"I scanned penalty strengths because more regularization is not always better. San Diego’s twenty-neuron model shows a spike in validation error at alpha 0.01, followed by lower errors. Changing the penalty can lead training to different fits, and initialization matters. This shows sensitivity to settings, rather than a rule that twenty neurons always performs poorly.\n\nPlanned narration: 25 seconds.\nThis slide belongs to the five-minute main presentation.\n\nSource: week6_regularization_results/alpha_validation_mae.png. One initialization per point."}
