{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/987a14va","slideIndex":9,"position":{"left":1160,"top":682,"width":60,"height":24},"text":"10","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":16,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#596C7D","alignment":"right"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/a9gru9cv","slideIndex":9,"position":{"left":60,"top":160,"width":540,"height":50},"text":"What the results support","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":31,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#2375A6","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/baps3edg","slideIndex":9,"position":{"left":60,"top":233,"width":540,"height":335},"text":"Chicago shows greater temperature\nvariation in this sample.\n\nModel choices depend on the city\nand the prediction question.\n\nL2 helped one smaller network.\nThe best overall MAE did not improve.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":27,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#17324A","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/wby9wju1","slideIndex":9,"position":{"left":690,"top":160,"width":530,"height":50},"text":"What still needs testing","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":31,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#D77D35","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/xc7a5ov6","slideIndex":9,"position":{"left":690,"top":233,"width":530,"height":335},"text":"One training year and one L2 seed\n\nValidation both tunes and reports\n\nRelated temperatures on nearby days\n\nCalendar input omits daily weather","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":26,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#17324A","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/qdcri5wr","slideIndex":9,"position":{"left":60,"top":610,"width":1160,"height":62},"text":"Next: an untouched year, repeated initializations, and a sine/cosine seasonal baseline.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":26,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#17324A","bold":true,"alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/9zorbm","slideIndex":9,"text":"The comparison shows that model choices depend on the city and the prediction question. Our earlier forecast had only one chronological test split. The later monthly and L2 experiments used one training year, and validation both selected settings and measured their performance. Nearby days are related, and training includes later dates. Those results do not establish future forecasting improvement. With only calendar input and one seed for the L2 scan, I would next repeat initializations, evaluate untouched years, and compare a seasonal baseline using sine and cosine features.\n\nPlanned narration: 40 seconds.\nThis slide belongs to the five-minute main presentation.\n\nSource: Synthesis of Section 11 and later validation experiments. Full original figures follow in the appendix."}
